<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bitola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monastir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bitola is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelagonia">Pelagonia</a> plain of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, near the Greek border and the ruins of the ancient city of Heraclea Lyncestis. Under Ottoman rule, as Monastir, it was a major administrative and military centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Serbian forces defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> nearby in the Battle of Monastir in 1912, and it is now the country's second city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bitola.jpg" alt="Bitola" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tommyy882 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ambientalna_ulica_Marsal_Tito-Bitola_(10).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in Macedonia in the 7th century, in the region around Ohrid, Prilep and <b>Bitola</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li></ul></section>
